+# The Daily Grind
+
+The homepage includes customer registration and sign-in with discoverable WebAuthn passkeys, profile editing, passkey naming/removal, and fresh authentication for sensitive changes. The browser uses SimpleWebAuthn; the Node backend verifies challenges, origin, relying-party ID, signatures, counters, and user verification. No passwords or biometric data are stored.
+
+Requires Node 22.13 or later. Install with `pnpm install`, then run `pnpm build` to bundle the browser assets. `pnpm test` runs account-service tests without opening a port or importing the server entrypoint. The generated `public/account.js` is committed so the checkout can be served without a build step; rebuild after changing `account-client.js`.
+
+For deployment, `pnpm start` runs the Node backend and serves the homepage and assets. Set `APP_ORIGIN` to the exact public HTTPS origin, without a trailing slash (for example `https://coffee.example.com`), `PORT` to the listening port, and `DATA_DIR` to a persistent private directory. The default origin is `http://localhost:11222`. Forward the public origin unchanged through your reverse proxy. Passkeys are scoped to that hostname; changing it prevents existing passkeys from being used. TLS should terminate at the deployment proxy. Static hosting of `dist/` displays the UI but cannot authenticate customers; deploy the Node service for account functionality.
+
+SQLite stores accounts, public credential keys, one-time five-minute challenges, and hashed seven-day session tokens. Back up the data directory and restrict filesystem access. Sessions use HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookies, with Secure and a __Host- prefix over HTTPS. Mutations require the configured Origin and are rate limited by socket IP. Adjust rate limits for your deployment proxy and traffic. A single Node process with persistent storage is the supported deployment model.
+
+Creating an account requires saving a passkey successfully. Sign-in can use a saved or synced passkey, a nearby phone, or a security key. Changes to profiles or passkeys require authentication within the last five minutes. Removing a passkey signs out other sessions; the last passkey cannot be removed. Email is profile data, **not verified identity or an account recovery channel**. Keep a backup passkey: there is no email/password recovery flow.
+
+Implementation follows the [SimpleWebAuthn server documentation](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server) and [passkey guidance](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/advanced/passkeys).
diff --git a/account-client.js b/account-client.js
